're all agreeing  to use the fast Fourier transform basis  and that means that if I tell you that I  need coefficients five seventeen and  thirty nine we all know what modes what  kind of columns of psy those correspond  to okay so you all have to agree ahead  of time what basis you're using for  compression JPEG uses the the DFT basis  or the fast Fourier transform basis JPEG  2000 uses wavelet basis and you have to  tell in when you save your file you have  to say what wavelet basis you're using  are using koi Flitz or you know horror  transform wavelets or you have to decide  and tell the person you're sending that  picture - what's the decoder ring what's  the basis that they need to go back to  real space okay so that's the idea of  sparsity is that you can write your your  signal X and some new basis and it  mostly contains zero and I'm just going  to write that over here because we're  going to use this later we're gonna say  X our original signal equals sy times s  where again where s is sparse and we're  gonna call sy a universal basis so I'm  gonna I'm gonna kind of write this down  for you this is what I mean I'm gonna  call this a universal basis and what I  mean by a universal basis this is an N  by n humongous square matrix okay that  square matrix let's say these are  megapixel images this is a million by  million basis and to some extent we this  basis being universal gives us the  flexibility of representing any natural  image sparsely so this doesn't have to  this could be an image of a face this  could be an image of you know a remote  control it could be an image of a dog a  cat a coffee cup you name it it can be  represented in this Fourier basis and  chances are if it's a natural image it  happens to be sparse in that Fourier  basis okay now you can also have a  tailored basis and we're going to talk  about this also in fact we already have  talked about this a tailored  basis that could be for example from the  SVD remember the singular value 