al and  you represent it in some kind of a  uniform a universal basis like Fourier  transform or wavelets then it's  representation becomes very very sparse  okay  and we're going to use this property in  in the next lectures too for example can  we identify if I only measured a small  subset of X maybe I only measured 10% of  the values in X can I infer what these  nonzero fourier coefficients are that's  the compressed sensing problem and again  this was not possible 20-25 years ago  but with advances in optimization and  sparse sparsity promoting optimization  we can now find those sparse Fourier  coefficients and therefore we construct  the full image even if we massively  subsample the pixels this is a this is  kind of almost like harry potter level  magic you know even compression is kind  of Harry Potter magic you take a million  pixels you throw away 95% of them and  somehow I claim that you can you can  still reconstruct the image even with  only 5% of the Fourier coefficients but  what's even more impressive is if you  didn't even measure those million pixels  to begin with so if I only measure a  subset of those pixels can I infer what  entries are nonzero so that's all coming  up that's called compressed sensing it's  super interesting really really rich  mathematical theory and that's what I'm  going to show you next  all right thank you

